{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And if with an iron instrument he struck him.</b> This does not speak of an unintentional murderer, for that is discussed below; rather, of an intentional murderer. And it comes to teach that one who kills with any object, it must have a measure sufficient to kill, as it is stated of all of them — ״אֲשֶׁר יָמוּת בּוֹ״ — which we render (in Targum): ״that is of a measure to kill with.״ Except for iron, for it is revealed and known before the Holy One, blessed be He, that iron kills with any amount whatsoever, even a needle; therefore the Torah gave it no measure to write in it ״that he would die with.״ And if you say the verse speaks of an unintentional murderer — but it says below: <b>Or with any stone by which one could die, without seeing</b> etc. — this teaches about those stated above that the verse speaks of an intentional murderer."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:13:52.566970269Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}